// a folder ROOT and a SIBLING in it, joining to one C symbol.
//
// `mangle_module_collision` next door states this class and does not
// reach it. Its two modules are `alpha/alpha.zen` and
// `alpha_beta/alpha_beta.zen`, which DESIGN.md's folder rule names
// `alpha.alpha` and `alpha_beta.alpha_beta` — the last segment doubles,
// so the two paths differ at the first component whatever the mangler
// does, and an underscore-joining one passes that test unharmed. The
// collision it describes is real; the file cannot express it, because a
// module is never named by its bare folder name in a symbol.
//
// A ROOT AND ITS OWN SIBLING IS WHERE IT IS EXPRESSIBLE, and it is the
// shape STYLE.md prescribes rather than an adversarial one — "siblings
// repeat the folder as a prefix", which is how `pkg/pkg_sub.zen` is
// named and how this compiler's own `parse/parse_expr.zen` is:
//
//   module `pkg.pkg`      + `sub_run`  ->  pkg_pkg_sub_run
//   module `pkg.pkg_sub`  + `run`      ->  pkg_pkg_sub_run
//
// Both modules sit under one folder, so the doubling that saves the
// other test cannot: the shared prefix is `pkg.pkg`, and what follows is
// `sub_run` on one side and `_sub` + `run` on the other.
//
// The two functions return DIFFERENT numbers and the two types carry
// different tags, because C takes a redefinition of a matching signature
// without complaint often enough that a link error cannot be the test.
// If the two share a symbol, one definition wins and this prints it
// twice.

sub_run, Sub_Box = pkg.pkg
run, Box         = pkg.pkg_sub

main = (env: Env) Res<i32, AllocError> {
    println("fns {} {}", sub_run(), run());

    r = Sub_Box(tag: 10);
    s = Box(tag: 20);
    println("types {} {}", r.tag, s.tag);
    Ok(0);
}
